Participant: Participant A-02 (synthetic operations lead)
Portfolio: Juniper Works / Product Alpha Q3
Interview date: 2026-07-13

Q: Which notifications help you act, and which do not?
A: A-02 valued a single daily exception digest because it named the queue and
owner. Individual status-change alerts were described as "a running commentary
on work I already know about." During a busy morning, six similar alerts made
the participant mute the channel for the rest of the day.

Q: Tell us about sharing results outside the product.
A: The CSV export downloaded successfully, but A-02 paused before sending it:
"I cannot tell whether this is the whole queue or only the current filter."
The filename contained a date but no filter or row count. They manually copied
the visible total into a message as a workaround.

Q: What would a trustworthy export show?
A: The first row or a cover panel should state workspace, applied filters,
timezone, generated time, and row count. The participant also wanted an
explicit warning when hidden columns or permissions change the export.

Initial codes: notification fatigue; export confidence; time-to-first-value.
Researcher note: This record distinguishes noisy event volume from actionable
exceptions; do not code a preference for fewer alerts as a request to remove
all alerts.
